A||||2||Johnson's85SE||e-brake cables back in slots||||5||
Z||000000||Johnson's85SE||11-19-2001||10:44 AM||||Hello,<BR> I just changed my rear pads, but ran into a quick problem. I couldn't figure out how to hook in the e-brake cable into the clip on the caliper. I put the spring back on, but the end of the e-brake wire is in the middle of the spring. What is the seceret to puting the cable back onto the caliper?What is the easyist way to compress the spring, when it is in place. Any help appreciated. Thanks, to the forum for walking me though the rear brake pads, (AKA Compressing the blanking piston)<P>------------------<BR>80's Import killer.<BR>[img]C:\My Documents\Stuff\Sports Cars\85fiero.bmp[/img]||||reg||5
Z||000001||tgowens||11-19-2001||11:42 AM||||Disconnect the e-brake cable at the rear of the cradle, hook the ends to the caliper locks and then reconnect the cable at the rear of the cradle -- you will have to adjust it all the way out to reconnect it (use a pair of vice grips and a 16mm wrench) - then readjust it once you have reconnected it. I usually turn it about halfway and then check the e-brake with the handle - you should have about 6 to 7 clicks on the handle when properly adjusted.<P>Be sure and use the e-brake when you park your car as this keeps the rear calipers in adjustment.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg"> <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Johnson's85SE||11-19-2001||12:44 PM||||Thanks, <BR> It was cold out here in Calgary on the weekend, so I didn't really look around @ the ebrake equilizer. <BR>||||reg||1
